h forces White to block at H6 to prevent a 5-in-a-row. This is a forcing move (Sente).\nAdditionally, there is a gap at I8 in the horizontal row 8 (H8, J8). Playing at I8 would create an "Open 3" (H8-I8-J8).\nCritically, if Black plays I8 first, White might play at H7 defensively, which would simultaneously block Black\'s vertical threat and interfere with potential diagonal connections. By playing H7 first, Black secures the vertical threat, forces White\'s response, and occupies the H7 square. The stone at H7 then supports a subsequent move at I8 by creating a potential diagonal connection (H7-I8-J9), leading to a powerful multi-threat attack (VCT).\n\nReason: The move at H7 extends the vertical line of three stones (H8, H9, H10) into four, creating an immediate threat of winning. This forces the opponent to play H6. Securing H7 is vital because it prevents the opponent from blocking this line and serves as a pivot point for a follow-up attack at I8, which would then form both a horizontal Open 3 and a diagonal threat.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "H", "row": 7 }'}
